  "notes": "West cashes 2 diamond tricks and exits in hearts, dummy's ♥A winning. South must hold his trump losers to 1 in order to make the contract.\n\nIf trumps are 3-2 or if either opponent has a singleton spade honor, South can succeed easily by playing trumps from the top. With careful play, South can also guard against East holding 4 spades including the ♠Q and ♠J.\n\nSouth should lead a trump from dummy to his ♠A or ♠K. While finessing spades on the first round would pick up 4 spades to the ♠Q and ♠J in East's hand, South would not be comfortable if the finesse lost. In this case, South would have to guess whether he should finesse spades again on the next round.\n\nWhen both defenders follow with small spades, South should cross to the dummy in clubs before leading a second spade. When East follows small, South finesses, guarding against East holding all of the remaining spades.\n\nThis line of play is not without danger. West might be able to win the second spade trick and give East a club ruff. So it is possible that the recommended safety play will defeat the contract when trumps are 3-2.\n\nThe chances of East holding a singleton club along with West holding a doubleton spade honor are not as good as the chances of East holding 4 spades to the ♠Q and ♠J. South should therefore take the safety play in trumps.",
